The C was obfuscated with digraphs and [38;5;10m<iso646.h>[0m spellings — [38;5;10m%:include[0m is [38;5;10m#include[0m, [38;5;10m<% %>[0m are braces, and [38;5;10mx bitor compl y[0m is [38;5;10mx | ~y[0m. So the whole program is: read two ints with [38;5;10mscanf[0m, print [38;5;10mx | ~y[0m and a newline.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Behaviors I deliberately reproduced rather than fixed:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
- Both [38;5;10mscanf[0m return values are ignored, so bad or missing input leaves the variable at its initial [38;5;10m0[0m. Empty stdin prints [38;5;10m-1[0m ([38;5;10m0 | ~0[0m), and [38;5;10mabc[0m does the same.[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mscanf("%d")[0m skips arbitrary whitespace including newlines, so the two values may be on separate lines or the same line. I read stdin one byte at a time with a single-byte pushback slot to emulate [38;5;10mungetc[0m, so no more input is consumed than the C would.[0m[0m
- Out-of-range input follows glibc: internally converted with [38;5;10mstrtol[0m semantics (saturating at 64-bit [38;5;10mlong[0m bounds), then truncated to the low 32 bits on assignment to [38;5;10mint[0m. That's why [38;5;10m99999999999999999999[0m behaves as [38;5;10m-1[0m rather than clamping to [38;5;10mINT_MAX[0m.[0m[0m
- A lone sign such as [38;5;10m- 5[0m is a matching failure that consumes the [38;5;10m-[0m but pushes back the space, leaving [38;5;10mx = 0[0m and letting the second [38;5;10mscanf[0m pick up [38;5;10m5[0m. Verified: both print [38;5;10m-6[0m.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
One thing worth flagging: [38;5;10mdriver[0m takes [38;5;10mx[0m and [38;5;10my[0m and computes [38;5;10mx | ~y[0m, which is asymmetric, so argument order matters and there's no bug to accidentally "fix" there.[0m[0m
